WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:01.110
- [Narrator] Three years ago,

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.150
luxury travel agent, Roman Chiporukha,

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:06.360
couldn't sell his clients
on a big opportunity,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.150
a chance to visit the
International Space Station.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:14.150
- I literally talked to
everyone for two years straight,

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.110
telling them about space.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.110
And initially, the response
was, "No, thank you,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.160
I'm not interested."

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:20.993
- [Announcer] Four.

00:00:20.993 --> 00:00:21.826
- [Narrator] But two years later,

00:00:21.826 --> 00:00:24.540
he sold a ticket for $50 million.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.060
- [Announcer] Ignition, lift off.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.340
- [Tourist] It's a great
looking photograph.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.750
- [Narrator] That trip to the ISS

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.730
is just one example of a trend

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.580
that some high-end
travel agents are seeing.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.500
Their wealthy clients want a dose

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.900
of adrenaline with their luxury.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.760
Here's what's behind the trend

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.003
and why some experts are
concerned about the risks.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.850
Tourists have been visiting
Antarctica since the 1950s,

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.200
but in the last few years,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.880
companies have built infrastructure

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.330
to make these trips more
appealing to the ultra-rich,

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.730
like these so-called Sky Pods.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.103
- And those experiences
that they wanna have,

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.120
prior to the pandemic might
not have even been on the radar

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.140
are now sort of front and
center and on the bucket list.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.050
The concept of YOLO, you only live once,

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.320
I think has really sort of
settled into the mindset

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.840
of post-pandemic travelers.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.340
- [Narrator] Tour operator, White Desert,

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.320
has built three camps on the continent,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.500
the newest can only be
reached by private jet.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.040
Clients can rappel down
cliffs, explore ice caves,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.010
and hike among fledgling emperor penguins.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.750
- So the bucket list trip

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.450
has become a trip to do immediately,

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.090
and those folks that have
done all of these things

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.860
are looking for more intrepid

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.086
and truly unique and
enriching experiences.

00:01:43.086 --> 00:01:43.950
(ice crunching)

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:44.940
- [Narrator] For these travelers,

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.140
the cost can be over
$100,000 for seven days.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.560
- Both the polar and
Antarctic regions are,

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.060
I would say that they are booming,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.340
and it's not just the regions themselves,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.310
it's the near Arctic and near Antarctic.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:00.420
- [Narrator] But the polar regions

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.790
are under threat from climate change.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.550
The world's ice is
melting faster than ever.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.640
- We're finding parts of the
ice sheet are melting now,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.790
and possibly going into
irreversible melting.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.080
- [Narrator] And in 2022,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.140
a paper found that each
Antarctic tourist corresponds

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.850
to about 83 tons of snow loss.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:19.950
(suspenseful music)

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.560
Some travel agents say that despite risks,

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.020
clients are looking
for the next adventure,

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.330
and more travelers are willing to spend

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.620
to reach these extreme
destinations without the hassle.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.100
The CEO of adventure travel
company, Untamed Borders,

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.980
said one client wanted to climb K2,

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.490
but didn't have the two weeks
it takes to hike to base camp.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.790
Since private aircraft
aren't permitted so close

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.680
to the disputed border
of Pakistan and India,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.184
Untamed Borders rented
high altitude helicopters

00:02:49.184 --> 00:02:51.360
from the Pakistani Army.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.840
What would've been a 14-day
trek took just four hours

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.340
in the air and cost $40,000.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.940
As clients take on more risk,
some are seeking extra support

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.392
from companies that
specialize in stepping in

00:03:05.392 --> 00:03:06.963
when things go wrong.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.620
Dan Richards' crisis response
company, Global Rescue,

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.310
is expecting more than
100,000 calls in 2023

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:17.370
with 2,000 missions and rescues.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:22.370
The company saw 221%
growth from 2019 to 2022.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:26.550
Richards warns that travelers
should take risks seriously.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.910
- I think that some of our,
you know, traveling members

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.940
have a very firm grasp on
what the risks might be,

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:33.780
but then there are others

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.700
that get swept up in the excitement of it

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.560
and don't necessarily consider

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.690
whether or not they're sort of a good fit

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:43.410
for what it might be that
they're encountering.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.570
- [Narrator] And on some
of the most extreme trips,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.090
tourists are beyond rescue.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.730
- If you're, you know,
10,000 feet under the ocean

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.673
or you're, you know, five
miles up in the atmosphere,

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.220
we're not only not gonna
be able to get to you,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.660
nobody's gonna be able to get to you.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.400
- [Narrator] Richards
recommends that travelers

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.080
do their due diligence.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.990
- It's wonderful that
adventure travel is booming, A,

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:07.650
B, I think people really
should do their research

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.260
and do their homework and
try and find the experiences

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:13.080
that are really good fits for them.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.940
- [Narrator] Despite the
dangers to themselves

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:19.290
and the planet, many ultra-wealthy
tourists are undeterred.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.123
- We keep wondering

00:04:20.123 --> 00:04:22.890
whether or not things are
gonna start to subside,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.230
and the boom just continues.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:26.940
So we're seeing strong growth,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.151
basically, across the entire sector.

00:04:28.151 --> 00:04:31.151
(suspenseful music)

